course: dictionary
lesson: 7877
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Rake is a good example: it can be a garden tool or an action, and it has other meanings too.
explain: «Rake up the fallen leaves.» Here rake is an action: to gather things with a garden tool. «Rake through the box for the missing key.» Here it means to search carefully. Read the words around the word to decide which meaning fits.
explain: Some phrases do not mean what their single words say. «Rake in the money» does not mean using a tool; it means to get a lot of money. «As thin as a rake» means very thin, and the rake is only the picture.
explain: Context can also keep you safe. «A rake left with its teeth up can swing its handle into your face when stepped on.» The sentence explains the danger, so you know why the teeth should face down.
fact: A rake is a garden tool with a long handle and a row of teeth or prongs at the end.
fact: To rake means to use a rake to gather things or make soil flat.
fact: To rake through something means to search it carefully.
fact: To rake in money means to get a lot of it.
fact: Always lay a rake with its teeth facing down, because if you step on the teeth the handle can fly up and hit your face.
exercise: «A rake left with its teeth up can swing its handle into your face when stepped on.» What is the safe way to leave a rake? | Leaning against the fence with the handle in the air | Teeth facing up so you can see them | Lying in the paddling pool | *Teeth facing down
exercise: Mum says, «Rake through the drawer for the batteries.» What does rake through mean here? | To get lots of money | To gather leaves into a pile | To water the lawn | *To search carefully
exercise: Tom is «as thin as a rake». What does this phrase mean? | Very tall | *Very thin | Very strong | Very wet
test: «Rake up the leaves before the rain comes.» In this sentence, what does rake mean? | To tread on | To earn money | To search carefully | *To gather with a rake
test: Which is a rake? | A bird that sings at dawn | A bucket for water | *A garden tool with a long handle and teeth at the end | A kind of rain jacket
exam: Why should you lay a rake with its teeth facing down? | *So the handle cannot fly up and hit your face when someone steps on the teeth | To keep the teeth sharp | To stop the rain getting in | To make it lighter to carry
